---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const desc = 'Free AI cluster fabric calculator: leaf and spine switch counts for a spine-leaf topology, uplink bandwidth, oversubscription ratio and optics count for GPU training clusters.';
const jsonld = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication',
  name: 'AI Fabric Sizing Calculator', applicationCategory: 'DeveloperApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Web', offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  url: 'https://theworldofai.org/calculators/ai-fabric/',
});
---
<Base title="AI Cluster Network Fabric Calculator: spine-leaf sizing and oversubscription — The World of AI" description={desc} current="/calculators/" jsonld={jsonld}>
  <h1>AI Fabric Sizing Calculator</h1>
  <p class="answer">A training cluster's network is sized so that every accelerator can talk to every other at close to full rate, because synchronised collectives run at the speed of the slowest link. A non-blocking spine-leaf fabric needs as much uplink bandwidth out of each leaf as it has downlink bandwidth to hosts, which is what 1:1 oversubscription means and why AI fabrics cost what they do.</p>
  <p class="meta-line">Formula and defaults last reviewed: 2026-08-31.</p>

  <div class="calc">
    <label>Accelerators in the cluster <input type="number" id="gpus" value="1024" step="64" min="2" /></label>
    <label>Fabric NICs per accelerator <input type="number" id="nicper" value="1" step="1" min="1" /></label>
    <label>NIC bandwidth each (Gbps) <input type="number" id="nic" value="400" step="100" min="1" /></label>
    <label>Host-facing ports per leaf switch <input type="number" id="leafdown" value="32" step="8" min="2" /></label>
    <label>Uplink ports per leaf switch <input type="number" id="leafup" value="32" step="8" min="1" /></label>
    <label>Uplink port bandwidth (Gbps) <input type="number" id="upbw" value="400" step="100" min="1" /></label>
    <div class="out" id="out" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </div>

  <h2>How the math works</h2>
  <p>Leaf switches = endpoints ÷ host ports per leaf. In a two-tier spine-leaf, every leaf
    connects to every spine, so the number of spines equals the uplink ports on a leaf.
    Oversubscription = host-facing bandwidth ÷ uplink bandwidth per leaf; 1:1 is non-blocking.
    Bisection bandwidth here is the aggregate leaf-to-spine capacity, the figure that governs
    all-to-all traffic.</p>
  <h2>Why AI fabrics are built differently</h2>
  <p>Enterprise networks are routinely oversubscribed 3:1 or more because traffic is bursty and
    uncorrelated. Distributed training is the opposite: an all-reduce has every accelerator
    sending at once, and the step does not complete until the slowest has finished, so tail
    latency and any oversubscription translate directly into idle accelerators. That is why
    training clusters are built non-blocking, why the network is a separate rail from storage
    and management traffic, and why lossless transport, InfiniBand or RoCEv2 with priority flow
    control, is used rather than best-effort Ethernet.</p>
  <h2>What this does not tell you</h2>
  <p>This models a two-tier topology. Above roughly the port count of one spine layer a third
    tier or a rail-optimised design is required, which changes both the switch count and the
    latency profile. Cable length, optics type and power draw per port are real constraints in a
    dense hall and are not modelled here.</p>

  <div class="cite-box">Cite this tool: <code>"AI Fabric Sizing Calculator." The World of AI,
    theworldofai.org/calculators/ai-fabric/.</code></div>

  <script is:inline>
  (function () {
    function n(i) { return parseFloat(document.getElementById(i).value) || 0; }
    function f(v, d) { return v.toLocaleString(undefined, { maximumFractionDigits: d === undefined ? 0 : d }); }
    var IDS = ['gpus','nicper','nic','leafdown','leafup','upbw'];
    function calc() {
      var endpoints = n('gpus') * n('nicper');
      var leaves = n('leafdown') > 0 ? Math.ceil(endpoints / n('leafdown')) : 0;
      var downBw = n('leafdown') * n('nic');
      var upBw = n('leafup') * n('upbw');
      var ratio = upBw > 0 ? downBw / upBw : 0;
      var spines = n('leafup');
      var optics = leaves * (n('leafdown') + n('leafup'));
      var verdict = ratio <= 1.001
        ? 'non-blocking (1:1)'
        : f(ratio, 2) + ':1 oversubscribed, so collectives will bottleneck at the uplinks';
      document.getElementById('out').innerHTML =
        'Endpoints: <b>' + f(endpoints) + '</b> &nbsp;·&nbsp; leaf switches: <b>' + leaves + '</b> &nbsp;·&nbsp; spine switches: <b>' + spines + '</b><br>' +
        'Per leaf: ' + f(downBw) + ' Gbps down, ' + f(upBw) + ' Gbps up<br>' +
        'Oversubscription: <b>' + verdict + '</b><br>' +
        'Bisection bandwidth: <b>' + f(leaves * upBw / 1000, 1) + ' Tbps</b><br>' +
        '<span class="sub">Roughly ' + f(optics) + ' transceivers plus cabling, which is often a larger line item than the switches.</span>';
    }
    IDS.forEach(function (i) {
      document.getElementById(i).addEventListener('input', calc);
      document.getElementById(i).addEventListener('change', calc);
    });
    calc();
  })();
  </script>
  <style>
    .calc { border: 1px solid #e6e4de; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; background: #fafaf6; margin: 1.4em 0; }
    .calc label { display: block; margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: 14px; color: #26262b; }
    .calc input, .calc select { display: block; margin-top: 4px; padding: 7px 9px; font-size: 15px;
      border: 1px solid #cfcdc6; border-radius: 6px; width: 220px; max-width: 100%; }
    .out { margin-top: 14px; padding-top: 14px; border-top: 1px solid #e6e4de; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.7; }
    .out b { color: #201868; }
    .sub { color: #7A8A9E; font-size: 13.5px; }
  </style>
</Base>
